Limit the memory one file can claim, and the links it can have.

Our own patch (AvP). It closes what patch 0016 left open: a few bytes of a crafted
setup header could make the decoder claim gigabytes of memory. Upstream's main limits
the codebooks in its unreleased rewrite (e99e46ed5c), and not the residue tables.
Each book and each residue's decode table now takes its worst-case cost from a 64 MB
budget before anything is allocated. All the links of a chained file share that
budget, because a seekable file keeps them all in memory. A stream that cannot seek
keeps one link at a time, so each of its links gets the whole budget.

A seekable file may have at most 256 links, because opening one recurses once per
link. When a chained file is refused, the setups of the links already read are now
freed; before, they were lost.

